  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $1.2B in contracts in FY2027 to date, across 2,712 awards with an average award size of $455K. Obligations fell 95.0% year over year, after several prior years with much larger totals: FY22 and FY23 were each $33.4B, FY24 was $29.9B, FY25 was $21.3B, and FY26 was $24.9B. Spending so far in FY2027 concentrates in technology and health services categories, led by Computer Systems Design Services at $242M and Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $102M. The Department of Health and Human Services also obligated funds in Custom Computer Programming Services ($92.5M), Other Computer Related Services ($84.6M), and several research, consulting, and facilities categories. The vendor list for FY2027 to date shows a mix of large systems integrators and specialty firms, led by ACCENTURE FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $69.5M, GENERAL DYNAMICS INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY, INC. at $62.9M, and WELLPOINT FEDERAL SOLUTIONS, INC. at $62.6M. These figures reflect fiscal year to date obligations; current-year totals are partial.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How big is HHS contracting this fiscal year?",
        "body": "The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $1.2B in FY2027 to date across 2,712 awards. Obligations fell 95.0% year over year compared with the prior fiscal period. Average award size was $455K, indicating many mid-sized awards rather than a few very large contracts. Prior fiscal years show much larger totals, with FY26 at $24.9B and FY25 at $21.3B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Where are the dollars going within HHS?",
        "body": "Computer Systems Design Services is the largest category at $242M, followed by Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers at $102M and Custom Computer Programming Services at $92.5M. The Department of Health and Human Services also obligated funds to Other Computer Related Services ($84.6M), research categories including $82.1M for physical, engineering, and life sciences R&D, and $40.9M for biotechnology R&D. Professional services, facilities support, and market research categories round out the top ten."
      }
    ]
  },
